{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.layout\n  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md §4):\n   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n   frame of the register machine through their layouts, and lower it\n   back.\n\n   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n   the kernels' image identities, R and H, which are checksums: A is the\n   only code identity.\n\n   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n   semantic-register evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n   `lower-state` is its inverse. The register kernel's return\n   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n\n   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n   semantic-register machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as register]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as stack]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.derive :as derive]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" (merge data (assoc {} :yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Static half: one segment\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- operands\n  \"The register operands of an A tuple, destination first, then its uses\n   in table order.\"\n  [t]\n  (into (if-some [r (code/rd t)] [r] []) (code/uses t)))\n\n\n(defn- register-operands\n  \"The `:reg`/`:regs` operands of an R tuple in table order (the `:live`\n   slot is `:data`, never paired).\"\n  [t]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [[[_ kind] x]] (case kind :reg [x] :regs x [])))\n        (map vector (get rcode/opcode-table (nth t 0)) (rest t))))\n\n\n(defn register-pairs\n  \"`[pc virtual physical]` for every register operand of every pc: A's\n   tuple and R's name their registers in the same slots (phase-5 §2.4).\"\n  [v image]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [pc]\n                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n                       (operands (nth v pc))\n                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n        (range (count v))))\n\n\n(defn- phys-maps\n  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n  [bodies pairs]\n  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n          pairs))\n\n\n(defn correspondence\n  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n  [h]\n  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n              acc\n              (-> acc\n                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n          (range (count h))))\n\n\n(def ^:private pushing-ops\n  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n\n\n(defn- stack-layouts\n  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [out node pending]\n            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n                      out\n                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n\n\n(defn segment\n  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        r (derive/register-image v)\n        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n    (merge {:vector v\n            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n            :bodies bodies\n            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n            :register r\n            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n            :stack h\n            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n            :pairs pairs\n            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n           (correspondence h))))\n\n\n(defn space\n  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n  [segments]\n  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n                  a (:address s)]\n              (-> acc\n                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n          segments))\n\n\n(defn segment-of\n  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n  [space a]\n  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n\n\n(defn- image-segment\n  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n  [space kind ident]\n  (let [as (get-in space [kind ident])]\n    (cond\n      (empty? as) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      (< 1 (count as)) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      :else (segment-of space (first as)))))\n\n\n(defn- inst\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:vector seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn- body-of\n  [seg pc]\n  (code/body-index (:bodies seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn phys\n  \"`phys(body(pc), x)`: the physical register R names for virtual `x` in\n   the body holding `pc`.\"\n  [seg pc x]\n  (let [s (get (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg pc)) x)]\n    (when (nil? s) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n    s))\n\n\n(defn live-out\n  \"`live-out*(p)`: the union of `L(q)` over p's actual successors (empty\n   for a tail call).\"\n  [seg p]\n  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n          #{}\n          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n\n\n(defn register-live\n  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n\n\n(defn virt\n  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n   refused.\"\n  [seg p s]\n  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n    (cond\n      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n\n\n(defn stack-layout\n  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n\n\n(defn chain\n  \"The closure pcs owning the body that holds `pc`, outermost first.\"\n  [seg pc]\n  (loop [b (body-of seg pc) out ()]\n    (if-let [owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) b))]\n      (recur (body-of seg owner) (conj out owner))\n      (vec out))))\n\n\n(defn- params\n  [seg c]\n  (nth (inst seg c) 2))\n\n\n(defn env-of\n  \"§4.4 forward: `E` at `pc` from the positional frames `F` (outermost\n   first, values already canonical), the task's free environment and the\n   activation's module store.\"\n  [seg pc free-env frames store-of]\n  (let [cs (chain seg pc)]\n    (when-not (= (count cs) (count frames))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :frame-depth {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/depth (count frames)}))\n    (cond-> (reduce (fn [e [c f]] (merge e (engine/bind-params (params seg c) f)))\n                    (or free-env {})\n                    (map vector cs frames))\n      (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n\n\n(defn frames-of\n  \"§4.4 inverse: `F[i] = (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))` over the chain at\n   `pc`. An outer value a shadowing inner binder hides reads as the inner\n   one; the canonical frame never held it.\"\n  [seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [c] (mapv #(get env %) (params seg c))) (chain seg pc)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Kernel dispatch\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn kernel-kind\n  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n    (cond\n      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n\n\n(defn- image-pc\n  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of absolute kernel pc `pc`.\"\n  [kind k space pc]\n  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n\n\n(defn offset\n  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n  [kind k seg]\n  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private parking-ops\n  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n\n\n(defn- safepoint!\n  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  [seg pc tail?]\n  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n    (when-not (and t\n                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n                   (if tail?\n                     (code/tail-call? t)\n                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n\n\n(defn- h->a\n  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n  [seg hpc]\n  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n\n\n(defn- a->h\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n;; :segment as A.\n\n(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n\n\n(defn- walk-value\n  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n   closures and continuations.\"\n  [f x]\n  (cond\n    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-closure\n  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n   :entry :segment :env}`: `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's frames.\"\n  [cx c]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n        ps (params seg owner)]\n    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    {:type :closure :params ps :entry entry :segment (:address seg)\n     :env (env-of seg owner (:free-env cx)\n                  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) (:frames c))\n                  (:store-of c))}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-value*\n  [cx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner cx) (canonical-closure cx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner cx) (assoc (lift-state cx (values/payload x)) :type :reified-continuation))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn canonical-value\n  \"Kernel value `x` with every positional closure and kernel continuation\n   reconstructed canonically (cx: a lift context).\"\n  [cx x]\n  (walk-value #(canonical-value* cx %) x))\n\n\n(defn- address-of\n  [lx seg-id]\n  (let [a ((:address lx) seg-id)]\n    (when (nil? a) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg-id}))\n    a))\n\n\n(defn- positional-closure\n  \"The inverse of canonical-closure: the entry's absolute pc in the\n   receiving kernel, the frames read back from `env` by the owner's chain.\"\n  [lx c]\n  (let [seg (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment c)))\n        entry (:entry c)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env c))]\n    (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)) (= (vec (:params c)) (vec (params seg owner))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    (cond-> {:type :closure :arity (count (:params c))\n             :body-pc (+ (offset (:kind lx) (:kernel lx) seg) (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (a->h seg entry) entry))\n             :frames (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg owner (:env c)))}\n      (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n\n\n(defn- positional-value*\n  [lx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner lx) (positional-closure lx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner lx) (merge {:type :reified-continuation} (lower-state lx (values/payload x))))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn positional-value\n  \"Canonical value `x` with every closure and continuation lowered into\n   the receiving kernel (lx: a lower context).\"\n  [lx x]\n  (walk-value #(positional-value* lx %) x))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lift: a kernel payload as the canonical act or tail\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private kernel-keys\n  \"The register payload keys of either kernel; every other key of an\n   entry is the engine's and travels in the pending.\"\n  #{:segment :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live :continuation :dest :resume-mode\n    :format :hash :image :images :store-of :stack})\n\n\n(defn- canonical-env\n  [cx seg pc frames store-of]\n  (env-of seg pc (:free-env cx) (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) frames) store-of))\n\n\n(defn- register-window\n  \"§4.2 step 4: `{virt(site, s) V}` over the entry's `[s V]` pairs.\"\n  [cx seg site regs]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) regs))\n\n\n(defn- register-kframe\n  \"§4.2 step 6, one frame independently: its own image row and A, `rd`\n   from A, never the physical `:dest`.\"\n  [cx f]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:return-pc f))]\n    (safepoint! seg pc false)\n    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n     :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs f))\n     :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}))\n\n\n(defn- lift-register\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n        tail? (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n        _ (safepoint! seg pc tail?)\n        k (mapv #(register-kframe cx %) (:continuation entry))]\n    (if tail?\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site (dec pc) :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n       :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n       :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs entry))\n       :k k\n       :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}})))\n\n\n(defn slices\n  \"§4.3's K slices of operand stack `st` under return frames whose bases\n   are `bases`, outermost first: `St[b(i-1), b(i))` per frame with\n   `b(-1) = 0`, then the current activation's `St[b(m-1), n)`.\"\n  [st bases]\n  (let [bounds (into [0] bases)]\n    (conj (mapv (fn [lo hi] (subvec st lo hi)) bounds (rest bounds))\n          (subvec st (peek bounds) (count st)))))\n\n\n(defn- slice-window\n  \"`(zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice)`; the two have equal length.\"\n  [cx seg pc slice]\n  (let [layout (stack-layout seg (dec pc))]\n    (when-not (= (count layout) (count slice))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/layout layout :yin.k/slice (count slice)}))\n    (zipmap layout (map #(canonical-value cx %) slice))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-site\n  \"`[segment site]`: the A pc of the parking instruction before absolute\n   stack resume pc `abs`.\"\n  [cx abs]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) abs)]\n    [seg (h->a seg (dec rel))]))\n\n\n(defn- lift-stack\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [st (vec (:stack entry))\n        frames (vec (:continuation entry))\n        bases (mapv :stack-base frames)\n        _ (when-not (and (every? integer? bases) (apply <= 0 (conj bases (count st))))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-base {:yin.k/bases bases}))\n        parts (slices st bases)\n        [seg site] (stack-site cx (:pc entry))\n        tail? (code/tail-call? (inst seg site))\n        _ (safepoint! seg (inc site) tail?)\n        k (mapv (fn [f slice]\n                  (let [[fseg fsite] (stack-site cx (:return-pc f))\n                        pc (inc fsite)]\n                    (safepoint! fseg pc false)\n                    {:type :return :segment (:address fseg) :pc pc\n                     :env (canonical-env cx fseg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n                     :window (slice-window cx fseg pc slice)\n                     :rd (code/rd (inst fseg fsite))}))\n                frames parts)]\n    (if tail?\n      ;; frozen §2.4: the current frames, store-of and slice are dead\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site site :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      (let [pc (inc site)]\n        {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n         :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n         :window (slice-window cx seg pc (peek parts))\n         :k k\n         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n\n\n(defn lift-state\n  \"A kernel's wait entry, parked record or reified payload as the\n   canonical act or tail payload (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried.\"\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [fields (apply dissoc entry kernel-keys)]\n    (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(canonical-value cx %)))\n           (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (lift-stack cx entry) (lift-register cx entry)))))\n\n\n(defn lift-context\n  \"The lift context of kernel `k` over `space`; positional closures and\n   kernel continuations are reminted under `owner`.\"\n  [k space owner]\n  (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {}) :owner owner))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lower: a canonical act or tail into a kernel\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lower-frames\n  [lx seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg pc env)))\n\n\n(defn- payload-segment\n  [lx p]\n  (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment p))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-regs\n  \"`(sort-by first [[phys(v) V] …])` over window `w`.\"\n  [lx seg site w]\n  (vec (sort-by first (map (fn [[x v]] [(phys seg site x) (positional-value lx v)]) w))))\n\n\n(defn- register-k\n  [lx k]\n  (mapv (fn [f]\n          (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                off (offset :register (:kernel lx) seg)\n                pc (:pc f)\n                regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window f))\n                store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n            (cond-> {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :return-pc (+ off pc)\n                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                     :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                     :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (:rd f))}\n              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n        k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-register\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :register k seg)\n        common (cond-> {:segment (:segment k) :format register/format-tag :hash (:hash k)\n                        :image (:register-id seg) :continuation (register-k lx (:k p))}\n                 (< 1 (count (:images k))) (assoc :images (:images k)))]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off (:site p)) :pc (+ off (:site p) 1)\n                             :frames (get fill :frames []) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil\n                             :resume-mode :return-result})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window p))\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :pc (+ off pc)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                               :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (get-in p [:deliver :rd]))\n                               :resume-mode :write-result})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(defn- layout-slice\n  \"`(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`, every member present in W.\"\n  [lx seg pc w]\n  (mapv (fn [x]\n          (when-not (contains? w x)\n            (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :window-missing {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n          (positional-value lx (get w x)))\n        (stack-layout seg (dec pc))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-k\n  \"`[frames stack]`: each return frame's slice appended in order, its base\n   the cumulative length through its own slice (§4.3), `b(i) = b(i-1) +\n   |slice(i)|`.\"\n  [lx k]\n  (reduce (fn [[frames st] f]\n            (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                  pc (:pc f)\n                  st (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window f)))\n                  store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n              [(conj frames (cond-> {:return-pc (+ (offset :stack (:kernel lx) seg) (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                                     :stack-base (count st)}\n                              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of)))\n               st]))\n          [[] []]\n          k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-stack\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :stack k seg)\n        [frames st] (stack-k lx (:k p))\n        common {:segment (:segment k) :format stack/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image (:stack-id seg)\n                :continuation frames}]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (:site p)) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n                             :stack st :frames (get fill :frames [])})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :stack (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window p)))})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private canonical-keys\n  #{:segment :pc :site :env :window :k :deliver :type :id})\n\n\n(defn lower-state\n  \"Canonical act or tail payload `p` as the receiving kernel's entry\n   (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried. `fill` gives a lowered tail's\n   `:frames`/`:store-of` (default `[]`/nil), which the return transition\n   replaces unread (§5.6 item 8).\"\n  ([lx p] (lower-state lx p {}))\n  ([lx p fill]\n   (let [fields (apply dissoc p canonical-keys)]\n     (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(positional-value lx %)))\n            (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (lower-stack lx p fill) (lower-register lx p fill))))))\n\n\n(defn lower-context\n  \"The lower context into kernel `k` over `space`; `address` maps a\n   payload's or closure's `:segment` to A (default: it is A).\"\n  ([k space] (lower-context k space identity))\n  ([k space address]\n   (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {})\n          :owner (:owner k) :address address)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Tasks\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn canonical-task\n  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic-register machine `template`\n   (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n   by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n   through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n   kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n   by this phase's layouts.\"\n  [k space template]\n  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n  (when (seq (:installs k))\n    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked k))\n        value (:value k)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n                   (:segments space))]\n    (assoc template\n           :code code\n           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n           :resources (:resources k)\n           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n           :free-env (:free-env k)\n           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n           :store (conv (:store k))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n           :installs {}\n           :halted? (:halted? k)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n\n\n(defn lift-task\n  \"§7.1 for a de Bruijn kernel: `rows/lift-task` over its canonical\n   presentation. `template` is a fresh semantic-register machine of the\n   kernel's composition; opts as rows/lift-task's.\"\n  [k space template serve! opts]\n  (rows/lift-task (canonical-task k space template) serve! opts))\n\n\n(defn attach-space\n  \"Kernel `k` holding the derived image of every segment of `space`, in\n   A order (§7.2: derive and attach the image for each A).\"\n  [k space]\n  (let [kind (kernel-kind k)]\n    (reduce (fn [k a]\n              (let [seg (segment-of space a)]\n                (if (= :stack kind)\n                  (stack/attach-image k (:stack seg) vm/stack-contract)\n                  (register/attach-image k (:register seg) vm/register-contract))))\n            k\n            (sort-by str (keys (:segments space))))))\n\n\n(defn machine-space\n  \"The space of the A vectors semantic-register machine `m` holds, and\n   the map from its segment ids to A.\"\n  [m]\n  [(space (map :vector (vals (:code m))))\n   (fn [seg] (or (get-in m [:code seg :address]) (when (contains? (:code-aliases m) seg) seg)))])\n\n\n(defn kernel-task\n  \"Step 12 for a de Bruijn receiver: semantic-register machine `m` (what\n   `rows/lower-task` restored) as fresh kernel `k` of the same\n   composition: every image derived and attached, the free environment\n   set, every wait, parked record and value lowered through the layout.\n   Delivers nothing, runs nothing.\"\n  [m k]\n  (let [[sp address] (machine-space m)\n        k (attach-space k sp)\n        lx (lower-context k sp address)\n        conv #(positional-value lx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lower-state lx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked m))\n        value (:value m)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))]\n    (assoc k\n           :resources (:resources m)\n           :free-env (:free-env m)\n           :store (conv (:store m))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores m) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lower-state lx %) (:wait-set m))\n           :ready-queue []\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter m)\n           :halted? (:halted? m)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? m))))\n\n\n(defn- lexical-names\n  \"The names the chain binds at `pc` of `v`.\"\n  [v pc]\n  (let [bodies (code/body-ranges v)]\n    (loop [b (code/body-index bodies pc) out #{}]\n      (if-let [owner (:owner (nth bodies b))]\n        (recur (code/body-index bodies owner) (into out (nth (nth v owner) 2)))\n        out))))\n\n\n(declare state-prim)\n\n\n(defn- value-prim\n  \"The path to the first `:prim` row carrier value `x` reaches as kernel\n   payload data: through guest collections, a closure's lexical bindings\n   (its positional frames) and a capture's state; or nil.\"\n  [code x path]\n  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n    (let [n (second x) ss (:slots n)]\n      (case (:kind n)\n        :prim path\n        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (value-prim code y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector ss))\n        :closure (let [[a entry _ bindings] ss\n                       v (get code a)\n                       owner (when v (some (fn [pc]\n                                             (let [t (nth v pc)]\n                                               (when (and (= :closure (nth t 0)) (= entry (nth t 3))) pc)))\n                                           (range (count v))))\n                       names (if owner (lexical-names v owner) #{})]\n                   (some (fn [[s y]] (when (contains? names s) (value-prim code y (conj path s))))\n                         (partition 2 (:slots bindings))))\n        :reified (state-prim code (first ss) (conj path 0))\n        nil))))\n\n\n(defn- frame-prim\n  \"A frame's window and lexical bindings (§4.4: the free part is the\n   kernel's :free-env, never payload data).\"\n  [code a pc e w path]\n  (let [names (lexical-names (get code a) pc)]\n    (or (some (fn [[r y]] (value-prim code y (conj path :window r))) (partition 2 (:slots w)))\n        (some (fn [[sym y]] (when (contains? names sym) (value-prim code y (conj path :env sym))))\n              (partition 2 (:slots (first (:slots e))))))))\n\n\n(defn- state-prim\n  [code n path]\n  (let [kframes (fn [k]\n                  (some (fn [[i f]]\n                          (let [[a pc e w] (:slots f)]\n                            (frame-prim code a pc e w (conj path :k i))))\n                        (map-indexed vector (:slots k))))\n        [a pc e w k] (:slots n)]\n    (case (:kind n)\n      :act (or (frame-prim code a pc e w path) (kframes k))\n      :tail (kframes e)\n      nil)))\n\n\n(defn lower-defect\n  \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n   before anything is attached: install children (not carried by this\n   phase's layouts), and for the register kernel a primitive function in\n   the payload data of a wait or parked state (a window, a lexical\n   binding, or reached through them), which its restore refuses as\n   `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`). nil when it can.\"\n  [task kind]\n  (let [slots (:slots task)\n        code (nth slots 11)]\n    (cond\n      (seq (nth slots 12))\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n\n      (= :register kind)\n      (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n                                (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\n                          (some (fn [[p st]] (state-prim code st [:parked p]))\n                                (partition 2 (:slots (nth slots 8)))))]\n        (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :continuation-registers :yin.k/path path))\n\n      :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn lower-task\n  \"§7.2 into a de Bruijn kernel: decode `wire`, refuse what kernel `k`\n   cannot hold (`lower-defect`) before any attach, then `rows/lower-task`\n   into `scratch` (a fresh semantic-register machine of the same\n   composition: the carrier's steps 1-11 and restore) and `kernel-task`\n   into `k`. Throws the first refusal as ex-data.\"\n  ([scratch k wire attach!] (lower-task scratch k wire attach! {}))\n  ([scratch k wire attach! opts]\n   (let [task (rows/decode wire (merge rows/default-budget (:budget opts)))]\n     (when (= :task (:kind task))\n       (when-let [d (lower-defect task (kernel-kind k))]\n         (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" d))))\n     (kernel-task (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach! opts) k))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '120,380p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc; rg -n 'logical-positions|canonical-under|cross-engine|ordered-pairs|lane-cases|lane-lower|layout-refusals' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; sed -n '950,1350p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '120,380p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc; rg -n 'logical-positions|canonical-under|cross-engine|ordered-pairs|lane-cases|lane-lower|layout-refusals' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; sed -n '950,1350p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [out node pending]\n            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n                      out\n                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n\n\n(defn segment\n  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        r (derive/register-image v)\n        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n    (merge {:vector v\n            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n            :bodies bodies\n            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n            :register r\n            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n            :stack h\n            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n            :pairs pairs\n            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n           (correspondence h))))\n\n\n(defn space\n  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n  [segments]\n  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n                  a (:address s)]\n              (-> acc\n                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n          segments))\n\n\n(defn segment-of\n  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n  [space a]\n  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n\n\n(defn- image-segment\n  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n  [space kind ident]\n  (let [as (get-in space [kind ident])]\n    (cond\n      (empty? as) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      (< 1 (count as)) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      :else (segment-of space (first as)))))\n\n\n(defn- inst\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:vector seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn- body-of\n  [seg pc]\n  (code/body-index (:bodies seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn phys\n  \"`phys(body(pc), x)`: the physical register R names for virtual `x` in\n   the body holding `pc`.\"\n  [seg pc x]\n  (let [s (get (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg pc)) x)]\n    (when (nil? s) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n    s))\n\n\n(defn live-out\n  \"`live-out*(p)`: the union of `L(q)` over p's actual successors (empty\n   for a tail call).\"\n  [seg p]\n  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n          #{}\n          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n\n\n(defn register-live\n  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n\n\n(defn virt\n  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n   refused.\"\n  [seg p s]\n  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n    (cond\n      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n\n\n(defn stack-layout\n  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n\n\n(defn chain\n  \"The closure pcs owning the body that holds `pc`, outermost first.\"\n  [seg pc]\n  (loop [b (body-of seg pc) out ()]\n    (if-let [owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) b))]\n      (recur (body-of seg owner) (conj out owner))\n      (vec out))))\n\n\n(defn- params\n  [seg c]\n  (nth (inst seg c) 2))\n\n\n(defn env-of\n  \"§4.4 forward: `E` at `pc` from the positional frames `F` (outermost\n   first, values already canonical), the task's free environment and the\n   activation's module store.\"\n  [seg pc free-env frames store-of]\n  (let [cs (chain seg pc)]\n    (when-not (= (count cs) (count frames))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :frame-depth {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/depth (count frames)}))\n    (cond-> (reduce (fn [e [c f]] (merge e (engine/bind-params (params seg c) f)))\n                    (or free-env {})\n                    (map vector cs frames))\n      (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n\n\n(defn frames-of\n  \"§4.4 inverse: `F[i] = (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))` over the chain at\n   `pc`. An outer value a shadowing inner binder hides reads as the inner\n   one; the canonical frame never held it.\"\n  [seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [c] (mapv #(get env %) (params seg c))) (chain seg pc)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Kernel dispatch\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn kernel-kind\n  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n    (cond\n      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n\n\n(defn- image-pc\n  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of absolute kernel pc `pc`.\"\n  [kind k space pc]\n  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n\n\n(defn offset\n  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n  [kind k seg]\n  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private parking-ops\n  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n\n\n(defn- safepoint!\n  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  [seg pc tail?]\n  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n    (when-not (and t\n                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n                   (if tail?\n                     (code/tail-call? t)\n                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n\n\n(defn- h->a\n  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n  [seg hpc]\n  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n\n\n(defn- a->h\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n;; :segment as A.\n\n(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n\n\n(defn- walk-value\n  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n   closures and continuations.\"\n  [f x]\n  (cond\n    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-closure\n  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n   :entry :segment :env}`: `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's frames.\"\n  [cx c]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n        ps (params seg owner)]\n    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n11:   id under rows/canonical-under (law 2); the pending's reason is the\n18:   root id on all three lanes under canonical-under), law 5 (six ordered\n586:    (is (= (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under wire corr)) (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under relift corr2)))\n1387:;; Phase 6c: the cross-engine lanes (§5.3, §5.4 laws 4 and 5, §8.3 10-12)\n1403:(defn lane-cases\n1422:(defn lane-lower\n1451:(defn logical-positions\n1473:  (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under (rows/emit (logical-positions (rows/decode wire) m))\n1501:(deftest cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare\n1514:(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-canonical-equality\n1516:    \"cross-engine-canonical-equality\"\n1518:      (let [cases (lane-cases) n (atom 0)]\n1527:(def ordered-pairs\n1549:        recv (lane-lower to (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver)) wire attach!)\n1576:            ordered-pairs)))\n1579:(deftest cross-engine-lowering-bare\n1581:  (is (= 6 (count ordered-pairs)))\n1594:(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-lowering\n1596:    \"cross-engine-lowering\"\n1598:      (let [ran (atom 0) cases (lane-cases)]\n1628:(deftest layout-refusals\n1664:          d (error #(lane-lower :register {:attach-stream attach!} wire attach!))]\n          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n          task (root-task wire)\n          env-path (conj wait-act 2 0)\n          edit (fn [f] (rows/emit-numbered (update-at task env-path #(rebuild % (fn [ps] (f (into {} ps))))) {}))]\n      (is (= [:lit 1] (second (first (filter #(= 'x (first %)) (pairs-of (get-in task [:slots 13 0 :slots 0 :slots 2 :slots 0]))))))\n          \"the lexical x is 1 at the read\")\n      (doseq [[label f] [[\"the unshadowed y changed\" #(assoc % 'y [:lit 8])]\n                         [\"the lexical x removed\" #(dissoc % 'x)]]]\n        (testing label\n          (let [[r attaches] (lowered toy (edit f))]\n            (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :free-env-mismatch] [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r)]) (pr-str r))\n            (is (zero? attaches)))))\n      (testing \"the lexical x changed trips nothing\"\n        (let [[r] (lowered toy (edit #(assoc % 'x [:lit 98])))]\n          (is (vm/blocked? r) (pr-str r))))))\n  (testing \"lift refusals, before any stream is served\"\n    (let [counting (fn []\n                     (let [n (atom 0) {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})]\n                       [(fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h)) n]))\n          stepped (vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/lit 42)))\n          m0 (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/v 'stream/poll!) (s/v 'c)))\n          [sref m1] (engine/attach-resource m0 (s/ring 8))\n          [cref m2] (engine/handle-cursor m1 {:stream sref} :k1)\n          held (vm/run (assoc m2 :store {'c cref} :yin.k/gate :running))]\n      (is (= [:observe] (mapv :reason (:wait-set held))))\n      (doseq [[label m status kind] [[\"not at a safepoint\" stepped :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil]\n                                     [\"a held :observe\" held :yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch]]]\n        (testing label\n          (let [[serve! n] (counting)\n                d (error #(rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts)))]\n            (is (= status (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n            (is (= kind (:yin.k/kind d)))\n            (is (zero? @n))))))))\n\n\n(deftest first-defect-order\n  (let [[toy wire] (lift-bare 18)\n        task (root-task wire)\n        state-path [8 1]\n        state (get-in task [:slots 8 :slots 1])\n        ;; the park's window: rd added\n        tampered (update-at task (conj state-path 3)\n                            #(rebuild % (fn [ps] (conj ps [(nth (:slots state) 5) [:lit :x]]))))\n        census (mapv first (pairs-of (slot task 9)))\n        swapped (zipmap census (reverse census))]\n    (is (= 2 (count census)))\n    (is (= :window-self (:yin.k/kind (first (lowered toy (rows/emit-numbered tampered {}))))))\n    (let [[r attaches] (lowered toy (rows/emit-numbered tampered swapped))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells] [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r)]) (pr-str r))\n      (is (zero? attaches)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Acceptance 8: the two ported tests\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn parked-reader\n  []\n  (let [m0 (vm/run (load-program (machine) (with-stream (s/then read-op read-op))))\n        src (s/stream-of m0)]\n    (stream/append! src \"A\")\n    [(vm/run m0) src]))\n\n\n(deftest a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent\n  (let [[parked src] (parked-reader)\n        _ (stream/append! src \"B\")\n        woken (engine/check-wait-set parked)\n        n (atom 0)\n        {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n        d (error #(rows/lift-task woken (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h)) (lift-opts)))]\n    (is (seq (:ready-queue woken)) \"the sweep queued the woken reader\")\n    (is (= :yin.k/not-quiescent (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n    (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")))\n\n\n(defn two-callers\n  []\n  (let [call-in (s/ring 8) call-out (s/ring 8)\n        m (vm/run (load-program (machine {:call-in call-in :call-out call-out\n                                          :call-out-cursor (vm/mint-oldest call-out :test)})\n                                (s/then (s/call-node :op/echo \"hello\") (s/call-node :op/echo \"world\"))))\n        e1 (first (:wait-set m))]\n    [(assoc m :wait-set [e1 (assoc e1 :call-id :parked-9)]) call-out (:call-id e1)]))\n\n\n(defn answer-both!\n  [call-out first-id]\n  (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response :parked-9 \"second\"))\n  (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response first-id \"first\")))\n\n\n(defn sweep\n  [m peers]\n  (loop [m (engine/check-wait-set m) n 12]\n    (if (or (zero? n) (empty? (:wait-set m)))\n      m\n      (do (run! s/serve-mirror! peers) (recur (engine/check-wait-set m) (dec n))))))\n\n\n(deftest a-response-cursor-shared-by-two-waiters-crosses-as-one-cursor-cell\n  (let [[ref-m ref-out id] (two-callers)\n        _ (answer-both! ref-out id)\n        expected (mapv :value (:ready-queue (sweep ref-m [])))\n        [m call-out id] (two-callers)\n        {:keys [serve! toy peer]} (served #{:reader})\n        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n        task (root-task wire)\n        pendings (mapv #(second (:slots %)) (slot task 13))\n        own-out (s/ring 8)\n        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach! :call-in (s/ring 8) :call-out own-out\n                                        :call-out-cursor (vm/mint-oldest own-out :test)})\n                              wire attach!)\n        [w1 w2] (:wait-set recv)\n        _ (answer-both! call-out id)\n        swept (sweep recv [peer])]\n    (is (= [:ffi :ffi] (mapv #(first (:slots %)) pendings)))\n    (is (= [id :parked-9] (mapv #(nth (:slots %) 2) pendings)))\n    (is (= 1 (quot (count (:slots (slot task 9))) 2)) \"the two waiters name one cursor cell\")\n    (is (= (nth (:slots (first pendings)) 1) (nth (:slots (second pendings)) 1)))\n    (is (= (:cursor-ref w1) (:cursor-ref w2)) \"the lower gives both waiters one fresh cursor entry\")\n    (is (= (count (stream-identities wire)) @n) \"one attach per distinct stream identity\")\n    (is (= [(apply2/success-response :parked-9 \"second\") (apply2/success-response id \"first\")] expected))\n    (is (empty? (:wait-set swept)))\n    (is (= expected (mapv :value (:ready-queue swept)))\n        \"the receiver wakes the same waiters with the same responses\")))\n\n\n(deftest act-ffi-bookkeeping\n  ;; Row 24: row 3's bookkeeping record travels in the parked table under\n  ;; its call id, and the validator accepts it in context :parked.\n  (let [world (bare 24)\n        {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n        task (root-task (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts)))\n        [p state] (:slots (slot task 8))]\n    (is (= (:call-id world) p))\n    (is (= :act (:kind state)))\n    (is (= [:ffi (:call-id world)] [(first (:slots (first-pending task))) (nth (:slots (first-pending task)) 2)]))\n    (is (nil? (rows/validate-frames task)))))\n\n\n(deftest a-stream-shared-across-the-tree-attaches-once\n  ;; Row 10 with the link-request stream named from the root's store and\n  ;; from the install child's: one identity, reached by two tasks.\n  (let [m (:machine (bare 10))\n        child (get-in m [:installs 'host.mod :vm])\n        ref-in (fn [x] (engine/issue-ref x :stream-ref module/link-request-resource))\n        m (-> m\n              (update :store assoc 'rq (ref-in m))\n              (assoc-in [:installs 'host.mod :vm] (update child :store assoc 'rq (ref-in child))))\n        {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader :writer})\n        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n        task (root-task wire)\n        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach!}) wire attach!)\n        lowered-child (get-in recv [:installs 'host.mod :vm])\n        handle (fn [x] (get (:resources x) (:id (get-in x [:store 'rq]))))]\n    (is (< (count (stream-identities wire)) (stream-occurrences task)) \"the tree reaches one identity twice\")\n    (is (= (count (stream-identities wire)) @n) \"one attach per distinct stream identity\")\n    (is (some? (handle recv)))\n    (is (identical? (handle recv) (handle lowered-child)) \"both tasks hold the one attached stream\")))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Carrier defects the restore would meet: refused before any attachment\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn refused-before-attach\n  \"Lower row n's carrier after (edit task): [status kind data]; asserts\n   no stream was attached.\"\n  [n edit]\n  (let [[toy wire] (lift-bare n)\n        [r attaches] (lowered toy (rows/emit-numbered (edit (root-task wire)) {}))]\n    (is (zero? attaches) (str \"refused before any attachment: \"\n                              (pr-str (select-keys r [:yin.k/status :yin.k/kind :rule :reason :defect]))))\n    [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r) r]))\n\n\n(def install-path [12 0])\n\n\n(defn with-store-key\n  [k]\n  (fn [s] (rebuild s (fn [ps] (conj ps [k [:lit 42]])))))\n\n\n(defn closures\n  [f]\n  (fn [task] (replace-nodes task #(= :closure (:kind %)) f)))\n\n\n(deftest carrier-defects-refuse-before-attachment\n  (testing \"a reserved root store key\"\n    (let [[status kind r] (refused-before-attach 1 #(update-at % [6] (with-store-key 'yin/def)))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name :store-key] [status kind (:yin.k/role r)]))))\n  (testing \"a reserved module store key\"\n    (let [[status kind r] (refused-before-attach 18 #(update-at % [7 0 1] (with-store-key 'yin/def)))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name :store-key] [status kind (:yin.k/role r)]))))\n  (testing \"a closure whose entry names no closure of its code\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :closure-origin]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 1 (closures #(assoc-in % [:slots 1] 999999999)))))))\n  (testing \"a closure whose binders are not its tuple's\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 1 (closures #(update-in % [:slots 2] (fn [ps] (conj (vec (butlast ps)) 'zz)))))))))\n  (testing \"an install whose retained response is {}\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-response]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 #(update-at % (conj install-path 3) (constantly {})))))))\n  (testing \"an install whose response image does not load\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-response]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 #(update-at % (conj install-path 3) (fn [r] (assoc-in r [:image :value] []))))))))\n  (testing \"an install whose manifest names another module\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-response]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 #(update-at % (conj install-path 3)\n                                                         (fn [r] (assoc-in r [:manifest :yin.module/name] 'other.mod))))))))\n  (testing \"an install in a phase the engine never carries\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-phase]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 #(update-at % (conj install-path 1) (constantly :bogus)))))))\n  (testing \"an install whose child is not blocked\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-child]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 #(update-at % (conj install-path 4 2) (constantly :halted))))))))\n\n\n(defn exclusive\n  [task]\n  (update-at task [16] (constantly {:custody :exclusive})))\n\n\n(defn without-trees\n  [path]\n  (fn [task] (update-at task path #(assoc % :yin.k/trees {}))))\n\n\n(deftest carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree\n  (testing \"custody (10) alone\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/unsupported :custody] (take 2 (refused-before-attach 1 exclusive)))))\n  (testing \"the dependency closure (9) before custody (10)\"\n    (let [[status _ r] (refused-before-attach 1 (comp exclusive (without-trees [10])))]\n      (is (= :yin.k/unsatisfied status) (pr-str r))\n      (is (seq (get-in r [:yin.k/missing :yin.k/trees])))))\n  (testing \"the child's dependency closure before the root's custody\"\n    (let [[status _ r] (refused-before-attach 10 (comp exclusive (without-trees (conj install-path 4 10))))]\n      (is (= :yin.k/unsatisfied status) (pr-str r))\n      (is (seq (get-in r [:yin.k/missing :yin.k/trees])))))\n  (testing \"an install record (8) before the root's dependency closure (9)\"\n    (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-phase]\n           (take 2 (refused-before-attach 10 (comp #(update-at % (conj install-path 1) (constantly :bogus))\n                                                   (without-trees [10]))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The dependency closure: each free occurrence in its own context (§7.1)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn halted-on\n  [ast]\n  (vm/run (load-program (machine) ast)))\n\n\n(def ghost-fn (s/lam [] (s/v 'ghost)))\n\n\n(defn lift-outcome\n  \"Lift m: [the wire or the refusal's ex-data, serves].\"\n  [m]\n  (let [n (atom 0)\n        {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n        r (try (rows/lift-task m (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h)) (lift-opts))\n               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e (ex-data e)))]\n    [r @n]))\n\n\n(defn missing-obligations\n  [m]\n  (let [[d] (lift-outcome m)]\n    (when (= [:yin.k/unsatisfied :incomplete] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/discovery d)])\n      (get-in d [:yin.k/missing :obligations]))))\n\n\n(defn routed\n  \"closure c, its E naming module store mid.\"\n  [m c mid]\n  (values/closure (:owner m) (assoc-in (values/payload c) [:env engine/store-of-key] mid)))\n\n\n(deftest free-occurrences-are-answered-in-their-own-context\n  (let [m (halted-on ghost-fn)\n        g (vm/value m)]\n    (testing \"a carried closure whose only free obligation is missing\"\n      (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations m))))\n    (testing \"a module store the closure does not route to answers nothing\"\n      (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (assoc m :module-stores {'unrelated {'ghost 42}})))))\n    (testing \"a routed closure reads its module store, never the root store\"\n      (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (assoc m :value (routed m g 'scoped) :store {'ghost 1}\n                                                   :module-stores {'scoped {}})))))\n    (testing \"the root store answers a top-level closure\"\n      (is (nil? (missing-obligations (assoc m :store {'ghost 1})))))\n    (testing \"reachable only through the store\"\n      (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (assoc m :value 1 :store {'k g})))))\n    (testing \"another closure's E answers nothing\"\n      (let [bound (values/closure (:owner m) (assoc-in (values/payload g) [:env 'ghost] 1))]\n        (is (nil? (missing-obligations (assoc m :value bound))) \"its own E answers\")\n        (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (assoc m :value 1 :store {'a bound 'k g}))))))\n    (testing \"reachable only through a module store a routed closure reaches\"\n      ;; the holder runs in outer, where ghost is 2; k, found in outer,\n      ;; runs at top level, where nothing answers ghost\n      (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (assoc m :value 1 :store {'mod (routed m g 'outer)}\n                                                   :module-stores {'outer {'ghost 2 'k g}}))))))\n  (testing \"reachable only through another closure's E\"\n    (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (halted-on (s/let1 'h ghost-fn (s/lam [] (s/app (s/v 'h)))))))))\n  (testing \"a routed module store answers, travels alone, and the lowered closure reads it\"\n    (let [m0 (halted-on ghost-fn)\n          m (assoc m0 :value (routed m0 (vm/value m0) 'scoped) :module-stores {'scoped {'ghost 42} 'unrelated {'other 1}})\n          [wire serves] (lift-outcome m)\n          {:keys [toy]} (served #{:reader})\n          [recv attaches] (lowered toy wire)]\n      (is (contains? wire :yin.k/rows) (pr-str wire))\n      (is (zero? serves))\n      (is (zero? attaches))\n      (is (= ['scoped] (mapv #(first (:slots %)) (slot (root-task wire) 7))) \"only the routed module store travels\")\n      (is (= 42 (vm/value (run-beside (assoc recv :store {'f (vm/value recv)}) (s/app (s/v 'f)))))))))\n\n\n(def fresh-read (s/next-of (s/cursor-of {:type :stream/make :buffer 4})))\n\n\n(defn fed-73\n  \"Lift m, lower it into a fresh receiver, then feed the source's stream\n   73 and run both.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [{:keys [serve! toy peer]} (served #{:reader})\n        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n        [recv attaches] (lowered toy wire)]\n    (stream/append! (s/stream-of m) 73)\n    {:source (vm/value (vm/run m)) :receiver (vm/value (drive-peers recv [peer])) :attaches attaches}))\n\n\n(deftest discharge-follows-control-flow-from-the-resume-pc\n  (let [past (fn [consequent]\n               (halted-on {:type :if :test (s/lit false) :consequent consequent :alternate fresh-read}))]\n    (testing \"a free read behind the resume pc is no obligation\"\n      (doseq [m [(past (s/v 'ghost))\n                 (assoc (past (s/v 'ghost)) :module-stores {'unrelated {'ghost 42}})\n                 (past ghost-fn)]]\n        (is (= {:source 73 :receiver 73 :attaches 1} (fed-73 m))))))\n  (testing \"a free read after the resume pc is an obligation\"\n    (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (halted-on (s/app (s/lam ['p 'q] (s/lit 1)) fresh-read (s/v 'ghost)))))))\n  (testing \"a closure reachable code can still construct is an obligation\"\n    (is (= #{'ghost} (missing-obligations (halted-on (s/app (s/lam ['p 'q] (s/lit 1)) fresh-read ghost-fn))))))\n  (testing \"a backward jump makes earlier code live\"\n    ;; §3.1 admits no backward jump, so no carried vector has one; live-pcs\n    ;; follows the stated edges, never pc order\n    (let [v [[:const 1 false] [:branch-false 1 4] [:var 0 'ghost] [:jump 6] [:const 0 1] [:jump 2] [:halt 0]]]\n      (is (contains? (rows/live-pcs v 4) 2))\n      (is (not (contains? (rows/live-pcs v 3) 2))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Every store-of names a module store of its own task (§2.6)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn routed-beside-stream\n  \"ast's closure routed to module store scoped, with an authentic stream\n   in the root store so that an attachment is observable.\"\n  [ast stores]\n  (let [m0 (halted-on ast)\n        [ref m] (engine/attach-resource m0 (s/ring 4))]\n    (assoc m :value (routed m (vm/value m) 'scoped) :module-stores stores :store {'stream ref})))\n\n\n(defn without-module-stores\n  [path]\n  (fn [task] (update-at task path (constantly []))))\n\n\n(defn module-store-ids\n  [task]\n  (mapv #(first (:slots %)) (slot task 7)))\n\n\n(deftest every-store-of-names-a-module-store-of-its-own-task\n  (doseq [[label ast stores value] [[\"a scoped store\" ghost-fn {'scoped {'ghost 42}} 42]\n                                    [\"a present empty store\" (s/lam [] (s/lit 7)) {'scoped {}} 7]]]\n    (testing label\n      (let [{:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n            wire (rows/lift-task (routed-beside-stream ast stores) serve! (lift-opts))\n            [recv attaches] (lowered toy wire)\n            [r none] (lowered toy (rows/emit ((without-module-stores [7]) (root-task wire))))]\n        (is (= ['scoped] (module-store-ids (root-task wire))))\n        (is (= 1 attaches))\n        (is (= value (vm/value (run-beside (assoc recv :store {'f (vm/value recv)}) (s/app (s/v 'f))))))\n        (testing \", its rows removed\"\n          (is (zero? none) \"refused before any attachment\")\n          (is (= [:yin.k/unsatisfied #{'scoped}] [(:yin.k/status r) (get-in r [:yin.k/missing :yin.k/stores])]))))))\n  (testing \"a closure inside a module store's value naming another module store\"\n    ;; k lives only in outer's value and is routed to inner; stripping inner\n    ;; alone must refuse, else k attaches and fails reading ghost\n    (let [m0 (halted-on ghost-fn)\n          g (vm/value m0)\n          [ref m] (engine/attach-resource m0 (s/ring 4))\n          m (assoc m :value (routed m g 'outer) :store {'stream ref}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"The layout code follows the corrected cumulative stack-slice rule and takes return destinations from A. I’m checking the cross-engine comparisons for hidden gaps, and will test the mutants independently. The engineer reports exclusions from the required matrix that need an architectural ruling."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1387,1710p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; rg -n 'defn canonical-under' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '910,1020p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1400,1490p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1387,1710p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; rg -n 'defn canonical-under' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '910,1020p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1400,1490p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":";; Phase 6c: the cross-engine lanes (§5.3, §5.4 laws 4 and 5, §8.3 10-12)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def link-rows\n  \"Rows the de Bruijn lanes do not carry: a require installs a child and\n   attaches a module image in the kernel's own format, and the layouts\n   of §4 reconstruct no install child (canonical-task refuses\n   :installs).\"\n  #{7 8 9 10 11 12 17})\n\n\n(def lane-rows\n  \"The wrapped rows every lane executes: 15 of §5.2's 22.\"\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) rows))\n\n\n(defn lane-cases\n  \"15 lane rows x 4 segments, plus row 20 once: [row segment expected build].\"\n  []\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) (matrix-cases)))\n\n\n(defn on-lane\n  [lane build]\n  (binding [*lane* lane] (build)))\n\n\n(defn lane-lift\n  \"The lift of §7.1 on m's own lane.\"\n  [m serve!]\n  (if (= :semantic-register (lane-of m))\n    (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n    (layout/lift-task m (kernel-space m) (on-lane :semantic-register machine) serve! (lift-opts))))\n\n\n(defn lane-lower\n  \"The lower of §7.2 into a fresh receiver on lane, of composition opts.\"\n  [lane opts wire attach!]\n  (let [scratch (on-lane :semantic-register #(machine opts))]\n    (if (= :semantic-register lane)\n      (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach!)\n      (layout/lower-task scratch (on-lane lane #(machine opts)) wire attach!))))\n\n\n(defn logical-names\n  \"§5.5's explicit correspondence: each served identity to the resource\n   key the lifted machine holds that handle under. The fixture builds the\n   same program on every lane, so one key names one logical stream.\"\n  [m peer]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[id e]]\n                  [id (or (some (fn [[k h]] (when (identical? h (:handle e)) k)) (:resources m))\n                          (throw (ex-info \"A served handle the machine does not hold\" {:id id})))]))\n        @(:table peer)))\n\n\n(defn ring-names\n  \"{stream identity -> resource key} over every stream m holds.\"\n  [m]\n  (into {} (keep (fn [[k h]]\n                   (when (satisfies? stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor h)\n                     [(:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor h)) k])))\n        (:resources m)))\n\n\n(defn logical-positions\n  \"The task with each cursor position's embedded stream identity renamed\n   to its logical name. A ring buffer cursor carries its stream's\n   identity, which §5.5's marker substitution does not reach (reported\n   as a design defect), so the cross-lane comparison renames it under\n   the same fixture correspondence.\"\n  [task m]\n  (let [names (ring-names m)\n        k :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity]\n    (replace-nodes task #(= :cursor-cell-body (:kind %))\n                   (fn [n]\n                     (update-in n [:slots 1]\n                                (fn [p]\n                                  (if (and (map? p) (contains? names (get p k)))\n                                    (assoc p k (get names (get p k)))\n                                    p)))))))\n\n\n(defn logical-root\n  \"§5.5: the lift's root id with every lane identity replaced by the\n   fixture's logical name.\"\n  [wire m peer]\n  (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under (rows/emit (logical-positions (rows/decode wire) m))\n                                     (logical-names m peer))))\n\n\n(defn lane-root\n  \"Build on lane, lift: [root under the logical names, the lane's own\n   reference run].\"\n  [lane build]\n  (let [world (on-lane lane build)\n        {:keys [serve! peer]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)]\n    (is (nil? (rows/validate-frames (rows/decode wire))) \"law 6 on every lane\")\n    [(logical-root wire (:machine world) peer)\n     (reference (on-lane lane build))]))\n\n\n(defn canonical-equality\n  \"Law 4 for one case: the three lanes lift to one root, and each lane's\n   own run answers the reference.\"\n  [build expected]\n  (let [roots (mapv #(lane-root % build) lanes)]\n    (is (apply = (map first roots)) \"law 4: one root id on every lane\")\n    (doseq [[lane [_ ref]] (map vector lanes roots)]\n      (is (:halted ref) (str lane \" completes\"))\n      (when (some? expected) (is (= expected (:value ref)) (str lane \" answers the reference\"))))\n    roots))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 10: every lane row once, bare\n  (let [n (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row r build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (canonical-equality #(build identity) r)\n        (swap! n inc)))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (canonical-equality tail-effect-halt \"B\")\n      (swap! n inc))\n    (is (= 16 @n))))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-canonical-equality\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-canonical-equality\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [cases (lane-cases) n (atom 0)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)) \"15 lane rows x 4 segments + row 20\")\n        (doseq [[row label expected build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (canonical-equality build expected)\n            (swap! n inc)))\n        (is (= 61 @n))))))\n\n\n(def ordered-pairs\n  \"Law 5's six ordered pairs of distinct lanes.\"\n  (into [] (mapcat (fn [from] (keep (fn [to] (when (not= from to) [from to])) lanes))) lanes))\n\n\n(def unlowerable\n  \"[row receiver] pairs a de Bruijn receiver cannot continue: row 4's\n   retained FFI re-parks through the kernel's own call-out pair\n   (ffi/response-wait-entry), not the carried response cursor cell the\n   semantic-register kernel restores. A kernel seam, reported.\"\n  #{[4 :stack] [4 :register]})\n\n\n(defn crossed\n  \"Lift on from, lower into a fresh receiver on to, satisfy through the\n   source's streams: the run's observables and attaches.\"\n  [from to build]\n  (let [world (on-lane from build)\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)\n        receiver ((or (:receiver world) (constantly nil)))\n        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (lane-lower to (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver)) wire attach!)\n        _ (is (= to (lane-of recv)))\n        attaches @n\n        done (satisfy world recv #(drive-peers % [peer]))\n        _ (s/serve-mirror! peer)]\n    (when-let [f (:finish world)] (f))\n    (when-let [f (:finish receiver)] (f))\n    {:attaches attaches :wire wire\n     :halted (vm/halted? done) :value (normalize (vm/value done)) :trace @(:trace world)}))\n\n\n(defn cross-lowering\n  \"Law 5 for one case: every admitted ordered pair continues to the\n   reference. Answers the pairs that ran.\"\n  [row build]\n  (let [ref (reference (on-lane :semantic-register build))]\n    (is (:halted ref))\n    (reduce (fn [ran [from to]]\n              (if (contains? unlowerable [row to])\n                ran\n                (testing (str (name from) \" -> \" (name to))\n                  (let [got (crossed from to build)]\n                    (is (= (count (stream-identities (:wire got))) (:attaches got)))\n                    (is (= (select-keys ref [:halted :value :trace]) (select-keys got [:halted :value :trace]))\n                        \"law 5: the lowered run continues to the reference\")\n                    (conj ran [from to])))))\n            []\n            ordered-pairs)))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-lowering-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 11: every lane row once, bare\n  (is (= 6 (count ordered-pairs)))\n  (let [ran (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row _ build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (let [pairs (cross-lowering row #(build identity))]\n          (is (= (if (= 4 row) 2 6) (count pairs)) \"every admitted ordered pair ran\")\n          (swap! ran + (count pairs)))))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (is (= 6 (count (cross-lowering 20 tail-effect-halt))))\n      (swap! ran + 6))\n    (is (= 92 @ran) \"16 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 into a de Bruijn receiver\")))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-lowering\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-lowering\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [ran (atom 0) cases (lane-cases)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)))\n        (doseq [[row label _ build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (swap! ran + (count (cross-lowering row build)))))\n        (is (= 350 @ran) \"61 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 per segment\")))))\n\n\n(defn register-judge\n  \"The frame validator's verdict on register entry `entry` of m,\n   reconstructed through the layout.\"\n  [m entry]\n  (let [sp (kernel-space m)\n        p (layout/lift-state (layout/lift-context m sp (:owner m)) entry)\n        code-map (into {} (map (fn [[a sg]] [a (:vector sg)])) (:segments sp))\n        as-sr {:code (into {} (map (fn [a] [a {:address a}])) (keys code-map))}]\n    (:yin.k/kind (frame/defect (ft/canonical as-sr p true) code-map :wait {:free-env {} :ffi-call-ids #{}}))))\n\n\n(defn spare-register\n  \"A physical register of the entry's body outside its live set and its\n   destination that R gives exactly one virtual.\"\n  [m entry]\n  (let [sg (val (first (:segments (kernel-space m))))\n        site (- (:site-pc entry) (layout/offset :register m sg))\n        body (nth (:phys sg) (code/body-index (:bodies sg) site))\n        unique (keep (fn [[s xs]] (when (= 1 (count xs)) s)) (group-by body (keys body)))]\n    (first (remove (conj (set (:live entry)) (:dest entry)) (sort unique)))))\n\n\n(deftest layout-refusals\n  (testing \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\"\n    (doseq [lane [:stack :register]]\n      (testing (name lane)\n        (let [m (on-lane lane #(vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/lam ['p] (s/lit 42)) (s/lit 1)))))\n              n (atom 0)\n              {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n              d (error #(lane-lift m (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h))))]\n          (is (not (vm/halted? m)))\n          (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n          (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")\n          (testing \", its registers read as a payload\"\n            (let [entry (if (= :stack lane)\n                          (select-keys m [:pc :frames :stack :continuation])\n                          (assoc (select-keys m [:pc :frames :continuation]) :regs [] :resume-mode :write-result))\n                  cx (layout/lift-context m (kernel-space m) (:owner m))]\n              (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status (error #(layout/lift-state cx entry)))))))))))\n  (testing \"a register entry whose live was tampered\"\n    (let [m (:machine (on-lane :register #(bare 1)))\n          entry (first (:wait-set m))\n          live (:live entry)\n          spare (spare-register m entry)]\n      (is (nil? (register-judge m entry)) \"the untampered entry reconstructs a valid frame\")\n      (is (seq live))\n      (is (= :window-missing (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (rest live)) :regs (vec (rest (:regs entry))))))\n          \"a live register dropped\")\n      (is (some? spare))\n      (is (= :window-extra (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (sort (conj live spare)))\n                                                    :regs (vec (sort-by first (conj (:regs entry) [spare :x]))))))\n          \"a dead register added\")))\n  (testing \"a primitive in a window: the register kernel cannot hold it, refused before any attach\"\n    (let [build #(reader % (s/app (s/v 'conj) (s/lit []) read-op))\n          world (build identity)\n          {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n          {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n          d (error #(lane-lower :register {:attach-stream attach!} wire attach!))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n      (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\")\n      (testing \", the stack kernel holds it\"\n        (let [ref (reference (build identity))\n              got (crossed :semantic-register :stack #(build identity))]\n          (is (= [\"B\"] (:value ref)))\n          (is (= (:value ref) (:value got))))))))\n957:(defn canonical-under\nnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\nharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\ntheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\nlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\nlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\npair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\ntransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\nof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\ntakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\nthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\nabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\nand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\ndoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\ncursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\nand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\nlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\namended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\nlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\ntheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\nthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\nenumeration order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n   `:duplicate-param`.\n8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n\n### 6.1 Code\n\n**DECIDED** (rulings 1, 3, 4; phase-5 §7.6, §9.2; v2 amendment §4's\n2026-10-10 note). The root's `code` slot is plain `{A canonical-vector}`\nof `\"v4\"` vectors, verified to hash to their key and to pass\n`code/well-formed?`. **No native image travels**: a stack or register\nreceiver derives `H(A)`/`R(A)` with `derive/stack-image`/`register-image`.\n`requires` carries UCF §7.6.1's map **plus** `:yin.k/trees {A #{tree-address …}}`,\none entry per address in `:yin.k/segments`; the **complete association\nrule** is phase-5's: an absent entry **or an empty set** is incomplete\n(`:yin.k/missing :yin.k/trees`, discovery `:incomplete`), and a claimed\naddress alone does not establish retrievability (a known address whose\nread fails now is the medium's `:absent`, not a missing association).\n\n### 6.2 Rows, not datoms\n\n**DECIDED** (ruling 4). No vector is projected to datoms; the direct\nload path is the only path (§10.4).\n\n### 6.3 The free environment: carrier, source, ownership\n\n**DECIDED** by the review's finding and §4.4. The task's initial free\nenvironment travels **once, explicitly**, as the root's `free-env`\nslot, a `:bindings` row (`[S V …]` sorted). Every `E` in the task is a\ncomplete map and is checked by rules X1 and E2. On lower, the\nsemantic-register kernel receives it as the machine's base `:env`\n(`create-vm`'s `:env` option, `semantic_register.cljc:261`); a de Bruijn\nkernel receives it as its `:free-env` (`stack.cljc:418`) and\nreconstructs positional frames from each `E` by the chain (§4.4).\n\n**Where the lift gets it (PROPOSED, the gap the review named).** It is\n**never recovered from a current `E`** (§4.4). Its source is, per\nkernel:\n\n- **de Bruijn kernels**: the kernel's own `:free-env` field, \"fixed for\n  this instance\" (`stack.cljc:82`; `register.cljc:50`), read directly.\n- **the semantic-register kernel**: the evaluator keeps only the\n  mutable-current `:env` (`RegisterVM` field `env`, \"persistent lexical\n  scope map\", `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:23`; the old semantic\n  VM likewise captures the initial env only transiently in `eval`,\n  `semantic.cljc:874-876`), so no field holds the initial map. The\n  **composition owns it**: it is the value the composition passed as\n  `create-vm`'s `:env` (`vm/check-bindings!` of it, `:261`). The lift's\n  input contract therefore takes it explicitly,\n  `(rows/lift-task vm serve! {:free-env E₀})`, and refuses\n## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\n\nReviewer rulings (codex gpt-6.1-sol, 2026-10-10): F1, F4, U2, U4, U5,\nR1, R2, R7, C1 **ACCEPT**; F2, F3, U1, U3, U6, R3, R4, R5, R6, R8, R9\n**ACCEPT WITH CHANGES**, applied below; dropping `:yin.k/engine`\n**ACCEPTED**; the merged-`E` free environment **REJECTED**, replaced by\n§4.4/§6.3.\n\n### 10.1 To the frozen design (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`)\n\n**F1 — §4.2 row names (ACCEPT).** Replace \"`:regs` becomes `[segment-A\npc env-cref window-cref k-cref deliver-D]` for an `act` and a sibling\n`:tail-state [segment-A site k-cref deliver-D]` is added\" with: \"the\nframe rows are `:act [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref rd]` and\n`:tail [segment-A site k-cref]`, the kind being the delivery\ndiscriminant, so `deliver` is not a slot; `:window` is a keyed row\n`[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by `r` in which a nil-valued register is a\npresent `[:lit nil]`; `:kframe` is `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nrd]`; `:stack` and `stack-base` are removed; the root gains `free-env`,\nthe task's initial free environment (continuation design §2, §6.3).\"\n\n**F2 — §4.2 the validator (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** After \"and pending\ncompatibility with `reason`\" insert: \"The validator is the pure function\n`(defect frame code context task)` of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§3: `task` is decoded task context (the parked key, the live FFI call\nids, the free environment), never an engine; its rules are ordered,\nstructural checks first, current-window rules on `act` only, return\nwindows separately, and every `E` is checked to agree with the task's\nfree environment off the lexical names; it runs after canonical\nnumbering, as the carrier orders it.\"\n\n**F3 — §4.3 foreign engines (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** After\n\"`lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames, as\ntoday.\" insert: \"The register kernel pairs physical and virtual\nregisters pc by pc from `v` and `R(v)`, recovers every destination from\nA's call (its `dest` is physical), and resolves each return frame's\nimage and relative pc independently; the stack kernel corresponds the\nnon-push instructions of `H(v)` with `v`, slices its operand stack\n`[previous-base, base_i)` per return frame and `[last-base, count)` for\nthe current activation, and needs a tail-completion seam to deliver a\ntail state through `K` (continuation design §4.3, slice 6c-pre); both\nkernels' positional frames become `E` through the owner chain and\n`bind-params`, with the task's free environment carried explicitly. The\nAST walker is not a frame reconstructor.\"\n\n**F4 — §10 phase 6 (ACCEPT).** Replace \"the row carrier amended\" with\n\"the row carrier amended to body version 3 with one profile,\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, carrying canonical vectors\nby A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's free environment, never a native\nimage (continuation design §2, §6)\"; after \"through their layouts\"\ninsert \"(the register and stack kernels, the latter with its\ntail-completion seam; the walker is excluded)\".\n\n### 10.2 To UCF (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`)\n\n**U1 — §7.3.4 `:505-527` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace `:511`\n\"`:yin.k/carried` carries vectors, not batches.\" with \"`:yin.k/carried`\nand the body's code map carry **register-shaped canonical vectors keyed\nby A**, never batches, never datoms projected from a vector, and never a\nnative stack or register image: a receiver of either native kernel\nderives its image from A (derivations design §2-3). `:yin.k/requires\n:yin.k/trees` maps every address in `:yin.k/segments` to the non-empty\nset of tree addresses the emitter's `source-index` held for it\n(derivations design §9.2).\" **Replace `:513-527`** (\"Loading a fetched\nvector has two paths … nothing may depend on which path a conforming\nengine takes.\") with: \"Loading a fetched vector has **one path**: the\ndirect decode of the canonical vector into an image through the one\nvalidator (`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`). The projection\npath (vector → datoms → loader) is retired (owner ruling 2026-09-18;\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §3.4). The conformance obligation is\nthat the two AST projection lanes and the direct vector load yield one\nimage and address (`load_paths_test.cljc`).\"\n\n**U2 — §7.4.1 (ACCEPT).** At its head: \"Superseded for the\nregister-shaped machine by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.1 (two\nstate columns, `act` and `tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table\nbelow describes the stack-shaped machine, which leaves at that design's\nphase 8.\" Replace the `:yin.k/frame` example with §1.2's keyed form.\n\n**U3 — §7.4.2 (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace, in the opening\nparagraph, \"absolute stack depth and environment key sets at a pc depend\non the *activation* — a closure enters with the caller's residual\noperand stack and an environment formed from its captured environment\nplus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack heights\nat joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths\" with\n\"the saved window's values, the environment's bindings and the depth of\n`K` at a pc depend on the *activation*; the same pc is legitimately\nreached under many windows and environments\". Replace \"E, St, and every\nK frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value\" with \"E, the saved\nwindow, and every K frame's window, `rd` and env travel in the value, as\ndoes the task's free environment\". In the static map replace\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test'","aggregated_output":"Loading initial Timbre config from: :default\n\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\nWARNING: load already refers to: #'clojure.core/load in namespace: yin.vm.encoder, being replaced by: #'yin.vm.encoder/load\n\nTesting yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.ucf.rows-test\ncounterexample-search random values 250 states: {:numbered 248, :refused 2, :max-work 395103} failures: 0\ncounterexample-search frame-bearing 40 states: {:numbered 37, :refused 3, :max-work 125296} failures: 0\ncounterexample-search with children 20 states: {:numbered 19, :refused 1, :max-work 308801} failures: 0\ncounterexample-search incidence families 100 states: {:numbered 74, :refused 26, :max-work 421748} failures: 0\n\nRan 60 tests containing 3615 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
